\section{Discussion, Ranks of Apparition, and Future Work}\label{sec:future}

For a prime $p$, its \emph{rank of apparition} $\alpha(p)$ is the least $n > 0$
with $p \mid F(n)$. Identity~\eqref{eq:sds} implies the fundamental law $p \mid
F(n) \iff \alpha(p) \mid n$. Carmichael's theorem says that $p \mapsto \alpha(p)$
is almost surjective: every $n \ge 13$ is the rank of some prime. The machinery
generalizes to arbitrary strong divisibility sequences (SDS) $a$ with $a_1 = 1$.

\begin{conjecture}[Rank is multiplicative-coprime-additive]
For an SDS $a$ and coprime targets $d, e$ (each appearing), $(d \mid a_n \wedge
e \mid a_n) \iff \lcm(\rank_a d, \rank_a e) \mid n$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Rank of a prime power]
For Fibonacci (and any SDS with a law of apparition), if $p$ is prime with
$\rank_F p = r$, then for $e \ge 1$, $\rank_F(p^e) = p^{e-1} r$ unless a
Wall--Sun--Sun-type exception holds.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Pisano period divides a multiple of the rank]
The Pisano period $\pi(m)$ satisfies $\rank_F m \mid \pi(m)$ with quotient
$\pi(m)/\rank_F m \in \{1,2,4\}$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Carmichael via abstract SDS primitivity]
Any SDS $a$ with strict growth $a_n \ge 2^n$ and a uniform bound on
$\prod_{d \mid n,\, d < n} a_d$ has a primitive prime divisor for all $n$ beyond
an explicit threshold.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Appearance-set semigroup characterization]
For an SDS $a$, the family $\{\,\{n : d \mid a_n\} : d \in \operatorname{image}
a\,\}$, ordered by reverse inclusion, is isomorphic to the divisor lattice of
indices via $d \mapsto \rank_a d$.
\end{conjecture}

The single most concrete open problem is the composite tail of
Section~\ref{sec:comp}: prove $\primPart(n) > 1$ for all composite $n > 10000$ via
the $\varphi^n$ growth estimate.

\section{Conclusion}

The prime-index case of Carmichael's primitive divisor theorem admits a proof of
remarkable economy: one structural identity~\eqref{eq:sds} plus the observation
that a prime index forces $\gcd(n,k)=1$. This case is unconditionally established.
The composite case reduces, via an explicit and verifiably correct primitive-part
construction, to a single survival inequality, confirmed by exact computation for
all $13 \le n \le 10000$. The mechanism --- first appearances controlled by
indices through a gcd identity --- extends to the full theory of ranks of
apparition for strong divisibility sequences.

